        }
      ],
      "id": "j9x1mig"
    },
    {
      "id": "ilmxdxj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Need for Classification of Elements"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Dobereiner classified elements into triads based on similar chemical properties and atomic masses.",
        "Newlands' Law of Octaves showed that every eighth element has properties similar to the first.",
        "Mendeleev arranged elements by atomic mass and successfully predicted the properties of undiscovered elements.",
        "The Modern Periodic Table arranges elements by increasing atomic number, resolving anomalies in Mendeleev's table.",
        "Periodic trends explain systematic variations in atomic radius, ionization energy, electronegativity, and metallic character."
      ],
      "id": "x8v7tzm"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "oos1dss",
      "content": "• As the number of discovered elements increased, it became difficult to study them individually."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ucmq4kq",
      "content": "• Scientists needed a systematic way to group elements with similar properties."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ygctko7",
      "content": "• The periodic table helps organize elements for easier study and understanding."
    },
    {
      "id": "ydmed7e",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Dobereiner’s Triads"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3ckrgln",
      "content": "Johann Wolfgang Dobereiner (1829) grouped elements into sets of three called triads based on similar chemical properties."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "heq3f53",
      "content": "• Lithium, Sodium, Potassium"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "0qanx4h",
      "content": "• Calcium, Strontium, Barium"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "jid0upf",
      "content": "• Chlorine, Bromine, Iodine"
    },
    {
      "id": "6hpfcag",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Newlands’ Law of Octaves"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bonc9m1",
      "content": "John Newlands arranged elements in order of increasing atomic mass and observed that every eighth element had similar properties."
    },
    {
      "id": "88tabef",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Mendeleev’s Periodic Table"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3ti8hiu",
      "content": "Dmitri Mendeleev arranged elements in increasing order of atomic mass and grouped them based on similar chemical properties."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "88lxl00",
      "content": "• Left gaps for undiscovered elements."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1ajvvmo",
      "content": "• Predicted properties of unknown elements."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jb1wxat",
      "content": "• Grouped elements with similar properties together."
    },
    {
      "id": "0ear7b3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Modern Periodic Table"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "d9ow802",
      "content": "The modern periodic table was developed by Henry Moseley in 1913 based on atomic number."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "65x1vkx",
      "content": "• Elements are arranged according to increasing atomic number."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9p7u3lm",
      "content": "• There are 7 horizontal rows called periods."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2sk6w1t",
      "content": "• There are 18 vertical columns called groups."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6ldrom9",
      "content": "• Elements in the same group have similar chemical properties."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
